{"page_content": "Column elution method Based on elution of the test substance with water from a micro-column which is charged with an inert carrier material such as glass beads, silica gel or sand and an excess of test substance. The water solubility is determined when the mass concentration of the eluate is constant. The mass concentration of the test substance is determined analytically Flask method The test substance is dissolved in water at a temperature somewhat above the test temperature. When saturation is achieved the mixture is cooled and kept at the test temperature, stirring as long as necessary to reach equilibrium The mass concentration of the test substance is determined analytically OECD series on Testing and Assessment Number 29 - Guidance Document on Transformation/Dissolution of Metals and Metal Compounds in Aqueous media. and sensitivity requirements Applicable to essentially pure substances only < 30% : Used for low solubilities (< | down to 1 yg/I 10\u00b0? g/l) Organic substances, but not mobile oils or liquids Applicable to essentially pure substances and also Os, \u00ab complex substances. < 15%; down to 1 ug/I Use of fast stirring techniques (300-400 rpm) appropriate for higher solubility (> 10-2 g/l) test substances. Use of slow-stirring techniques (<100 rpm) appropriate for low solubility (< 10-2 g/l) test substances (Letinski et al, 2002) Requires equilibration study to determine the time taken to equilibrate the test substance and water Applicable to all metals and sparingly soluble inorganic metal compounds", "metadata": {"doc_id": "ace95d8f-2754-48b8-b304-5fb30d589c75", "content_type": "Table", "filename": "information_requirements_r7a_en_part_1.pdf"}}